      {
        "value": "Binary politeness distinction",
        "source": "wals-v2020.5",
        "note": "<div class=\"Example\"><table class=\"example\"><tr><td><p><i>khyed.rang.</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- Etymology: the constituents <i>rang</i> (self) and <i>tsho</i>(group) are transparent to native speakers; <i>rnam</i> is found asa noun in Preclassical Tibetan, meaning 'individuals'</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>khyed.rnams.tsho./ nga.rnams.tsho.</i></p></td><td><p>2nd pl. hon.</p></td><td><p>- the second form is based on the 1sg ordinary pronoun, thegrammar, however, does not indicate the rules of usage;</p><p>- all plural honorific pronouns take the form <i>rnams</i> whichderives from a noun in Preclassical Tibetan with the meaning'individuals'</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>khyod.(rang).</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. fam.</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>khyod.(rang.)tsho.</i></p></td><td><p>2nd pl.</p></td><td><p>-2pl familiar pronoun; based on 2sg familiar</p></td></tr></table></div>",
        "scale": "wals-v2020.5"
      }
    ]
